ZzzCharacter.cpp's per-frame effect switch, the MODEL_BUDGE_DRAGON case:

    if (o->CurrentAction == MONSTER01_ATTACK1 && o->AnimationFrame <= 4.f && rand_fps_check(1))
    {
        Vector(0.f, (float)(rand() % 32 + 32), 0.f, p);
        b->TransformPosition(o->BoneTransform[7], p, Position, true);
        CreateParticle(BITMAP_FIRE, Position, o->Angle, Light, 1);
    }

Four facts, and only these four are the monster's. Bone 7, which this rig calls Bip01 Head - so the number in the client is the head and is written here as the head, because a bone index is a fact about Monster03.bmd's ordering and the mouth is a fact about the animal. Action 3, which is MONSTER01_ATTACK1. Through key 4, which is where AnimationFrame <= 4 stops. And that the particle is MU's fire.

Everything else about what the fire looks like - that a spark lives 24 frames, is born at six centimetres and grows to near eighty, is thrown a metre a second along the facing and dragged to a stop, and plays the four frames of Fire01 once as it fades - belongs to BITMAP_FIRE subtype 1 rather than to this monster, and is in viewer/Breath.cs where the next thing to breathe fire can share it.

through_key is MU's own counter and not a time. The build converts it against this rig's key count for action 3, which is seven, so the window is the first four sevenths of the swing; see monster_effects in index.py for why it travels as a fraction and not as seconds.

The dust is the second, and it arrives by accident of C: this case has no break, so the monster falls into MODEL_DARK_KNIGHT's, which drops a BITMAP_SMOKE+1 every fourth frame while it is alive. Whether a dragon was meant to have the Dark Knight's dust cannot be answered from the source and does not need to be - the client does it, so a player saw it. It is dust rather than smoke because of what subtype 0 does rather than what it is called: it pins itself to the terrain every frame and opens out from 0.3 m to near 1.9 m, which is a puff kicked up off the floor and not a column rising off the animal. No bone, because MU emits it at the object's own position - its feet - and not off any part of it.

Still not declared: the hover - Position[2] += (-|sin(Timer)| * 70 + 70) in MoveMonsterVisual, which is this monster alone in the whole switch - is motion rather than an effect and belongs wherever the walk does.
